<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaston de Foix, Duke of Nemours</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1489">1489</a>–<a href="/years/1512">1512</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaston de Foix was a French nobleman and commander, nephew of Louis XII, who exchanged his viscounty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> for the duchy of Nemours in 1507. Leading the French army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1511">Holy League</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-league-of-cambrai">War of the League of Cambrai</a>, he won a series of rapid victories and the Battle of Ravenna on 11 April 1512, but was killed during the pursuit at the age of 22.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaston-de-foix-duke-of-nemours.jpg" alt="Gaston de Foix, Duke of Nemours" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philippe de Champaigne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Gaston_of_Foix,_Duke_of_Nemours_(1489%E2%80%931512),_by_Philippe_de_Champaigne_-_Palace_of_Versailles.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gaston was born on 10 December 1489 at Mazères in the County of Foix, the second child and only son of John of Foix, Viscount of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>, and Marie d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>. His grandparents were Gaston IV of Foix-Grailly and Queen Eleanor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> on one side, and Charles, Duke of Orléans, and Marie of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a> on the other; his only maternal uncle was King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france">Louis XII of France</a>. His elder sister, Germaine of Foix, became queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> as the second wife of Ferdinand II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as commander in 1511, aged 21, and was soon called the Thunderbolt of Italy for his forced marches and sudden offensives. In October 1511 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1511">Holy League</a> with Ferdinand of Aragon and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> to drive the French out of Italy. Facing the Swiss in the north, a Papal and Aragonese army under Ramón de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardona">Cardona</a>, Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in the south and Venice in the east, Gaston held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, reinforced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> and in February 1512 relieved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>, which he took and sacked.</p><p>Marching south through the snow, he threatened Ravenna and invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardona">Cardona</a> to battle. On 11 April 1512 about 23,000 French troops, among them 8,500 German landsknechts, with 54 guns, attacked a smaller army entrenched behind works prepared by the engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-navarro">Pedro Navarro</a>. French artillery provoked the Spanish cavalry into failed charges, and after an hour of fighting in the entrenchments two guns sent behind the Spanish lines broke their resistance. Leading a charge against retreating infantry, Gaston fell when his horse stumbled and was shot dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ravenna cost the French up to 9,000 men, and the loss of Gaston and over a third of the army fatally weakened them in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. When the Swiss and Venetians marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, his successor Jacques de La Palice fled with his army to the Dauphiné in June. Germaine inherited his claim to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre</a>, and Ferdinand invaded and kept its Spanish lands. An elaborate tomb from the workshop of Agostino Busti, never completed, is mostly on display in the Castello Sforzesco in Milan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gaston of Foix, Duke of Nemours” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1507 <b>Gaston de Foix</b> exchanged it with his uncle Louis XII for the duchy of Nemours, and it was joined to the Kingdom of France.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-narbonne">Viscounty of Narbonne</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mazères, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Battle of Ravenna, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.25,1.68&amp;year=1503" aria-label="Mazères on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gaston de Foix, Duke of Nemours"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5105 3876h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5105 3876h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.25,1.68&amp;year=1503">What was here in 1503? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
